1
00:00:04,520 --> 00:00:05,280
Two in the afternoon.

2
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:07,281
You have one article to read before a

3
00:00:07,321 --> 00:00:07,581
meeting.

4
00:00:07,901 --> 00:00:08,781
Just twelve pages.

5
00:00:09,102 --> 00:00:09,942
How hard can it be?

6
00:00:10,562 --> 00:00:11,702
You read the first paragraph.

7
00:00:12,343 --> 00:00:13,183
Then your phone buzzes.

8
00:00:13,563 --> 00:00:14,023
It's nothing.

9
00:00:14,063 --> 00:00:15,764
Just a sale on shoes you'll never buy.

10
00:00:16,604 --> 00:00:17,604
You go back to the article.

11
00:00:17,964 --> 00:00:19,085
Wait, where were you?

12
00:00:19,945 --> 00:00:21,205
You read the same paragraph again.

13
00:00:21,746 --> 00:00:22,186
And again.

14
00:00:23,046 --> 00:00:24,546
Your eyes reach the bottom of the page,

15
00:00:24,606 --> 00:00:26,527
but your mind is thinking about dinner.

16
00:00:27,247 --> 00:00:28,208
You open a new tab,

17
00:00:28,288 --> 00:00:29,228
just to check one thing.

18
00:00:29,948 --> 00:00:31,729
Forty minutes later, you're still on page

19
00:00:31,789 --> 00:00:32,029
two.

20
00:00:32,669 --> 00:00:34,150
You scroll back up and realize you can't

21
00:00:34,190 --> 00:00:36,110
remember a single thing from page one.

22
00:00:36,830 --> 00:00:38,071
Is something wrong with your brain?

23
00:00:39,251 --> 00:00:40,452
You may have heard a scary fact.

24
00:00:40,973 --> 00:00:42,695
Humans now have an attention span of eight

25
00:00:42,775 --> 00:00:44,577
seconds, shorter than a goldfish.

26
00:00:45,177 --> 00:00:46,619
It's been repeated in news stories,

27
00:00:46,679 --> 00:00:48,501
talks and ads all over the world.

28
00:00:49,321 --> 00:00:51,223
But when BBC reporters went looking for the

29
00:00:51,263 --> 00:00:53,085
study behind it, they couldn't find one.

30
00:00:53,786 --> 00:00:55,708
The report that spread the number didn't contain

31
00:00:55,728 --> 00:00:56,669
the research behind it.

32
00:00:57,270 --> 00:00:59,312
And nobody had measured a goldfish's attention

33
00:00:59,352 --> 00:00:59,912
span either.

34
00:01:00,673 --> 00:01:01,954
So if it's not a goldfish brain,

35
00:01:01,974 --> 00:01:02,615
what's going on?

36
00:01:03,035 --> 00:01:04,428
Let's collect some clues.

37
00:01:04,428 --> 00:01:05,125
Clue one.

38
00:01:05,125 --> 00:01:06,519
In a famous study,

39
00:01:06,519 --> 00:01:09,306
an app pinged thousands of people at random

40
00:01:09,306 --> 00:01:11,744
moments and asked what they were thinking

41
00:01:11,744 --> 00:01:12,093
about.

42
00:01:12,093 --> 00:01:13,486
Nearly half the time,

43
00:01:13,486 --> 00:01:15,576
forty-seven percent, they were thinking about

44
00:01:15,576 --> 00:01:18,015
something other than what they were doing.

45
00:01:18,015 --> 00:01:19,408
Reading is no exception.

46
00:01:19,408 --> 00:01:21,150
When researchers interrupt people reading

47
00:01:21,150 --> 00:01:22,544
a story and ask,

48
00:01:22,544 --> 00:01:23,937
"Were you on task?",

49
00:01:23,937 --> 00:01:25,679
the answer is often no.

50
00:01:25,679 --> 00:01:28,466
In several studies, around one in five times.

51
00:01:28,466 --> 00:01:30,208
And here's the strange part.

52
00:01:30,208 --> 00:01:32,646
During these moments, the eyes keep moving

53
00:01:32,646 --> 00:01:33,691
across the words.

54
00:01:33,691 --> 00:01:35,085
Your eyes are reading.

55
00:01:35,085 --> 00:01:36,478
Your mind is not.

56
00:01:36,478 --> 00:01:39,265
Your eyes even stop slowing down for hard

57
00:01:39,265 --> 00:01:42,052
or unusual words, as if they were on

58
00:01:42,052 --> 00:01:42,400
autopilot.

59
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:45,187
And readers who zone out more often do

60
00:01:45,187 --> 00:01:47,626
worse on questions about the text afterward.

61
00:01:48,106 --> 00:01:48,586
Clue two.

62
00:01:48,967 --> 00:01:50,147
In a 2015 experiment,

63
00:01:50,647 --> 00:01:52,929
people did a task that needed close attention.

64
00:01:53,689 --> 00:01:55,170
Some of them got a text message or

65
00:01:55,230 --> 00:01:56,811
a call on their phone during the task.

66
00:01:57,471 --> 00:01:58,172
They didn't answer it.

67
00:01:58,692 --> 00:02:00,013
They didn't even touch the phone.

68
00:02:00,733 --> 00:02:02,694
Yet they made noticeably more mistakes,

69
00:02:03,395 --> 00:02:05,176
about as many as people who actually used

70
00:02:05,196 --> 00:02:05,716
their phones.

71
00:02:06,296 --> 00:02:06,796
Clue three.

72
00:02:07,517 --> 00:02:09,338
Every time you switch from one task to

73
00:02:09,379 --> 00:02:12,021
another, your brain pays a small time cost

74
00:02:12,061 --> 00:02:12,941
to change its rules.

75
00:02:13,762 --> 00:02:15,083
The more complex the tasks,

76
00:02:15,704 --> 00:02:16,664
the bigger the cost.

77
00:02:17,525 --> 00:02:19,447
And when you switch away from something unfinished,

78
00:02:19,967 --> 00:02:21,768
part of your attention stays stuck on it.

79
00:02:22,449 --> 00:02:24,951
Researchers call this attention residue.

80
00:02:25,471 --> 00:02:26,031
Clue four.

81
00:02:26,712 --> 00:02:29,013
A large review compared reading on paper with

82
00:02:29,173 --> 00:02:30,053
reading on screens.

83
00:02:30,753 --> 00:02:32,454
On average, people understood informational

84
00:02:32,514 --> 00:02:34,055
texts a little better on paper.

85
00:02:34,755 --> 00:02:36,256
The gap was bigger when they were in

86
00:02:36,276 --> 00:02:36,616
a hurry.

87
00:02:37,636 --> 00:02:39,657
And people reading on screens were more likely

88
00:02:39,697 --> 00:02:41,998
to overestimate how much they'd understood.

89
00:02:42,558 --> 00:02:44,079
So why can't you focus on reading?

90
00:02:45,099 --> 00:02:48,121
A: your attention span has shrunk to less

91
00:02:48,181 --> 00:02:49,142
than a goldfish's.

92
00:02:49,782 --> 00:02:52,263
B: your phone has permanently damaged your

93
00:02:52,303 --> 00:02:52,643
brain.

94
00:02:53,164 --> 00:02:55,705
Or C: your attention is like a spotlight

95
00:02:55,745 --> 00:02:56,966
that keeps getting pulled away,

96
00:02:57,446 --> 00:02:59,267
and every trip back costs you a little.

97
00:02:59,787 --> 00:03:01,113
Pause the video and take a guess.

98
00:03:10,167 --> 00:03:10,808
The key is this.

99
00:03:11,308 --> 00:03:13,610
Attention isn't a tank that slowly runs empty.

100
00:03:14,310 --> 00:03:15,711
It's more like a spotlight that you keep

101
00:03:15,831 --> 00:03:17,573
aiming, and that keeps getting pulled off

102
00:03:17,613 --> 00:03:17,973
target.

103
00:03:18,754 --> 00:03:20,275
To see why reading suffers so much,

104
00:03:20,655 --> 00:03:22,457
let's look at what reading asks of that

105
00:03:22,537 --> 00:03:22,997
spotlight.

106
00:03:23,597 --> 00:03:25,519
Reading isn't just seeing words.

107
00:03:26,099 --> 00:03:27,180
To understand a paragraph,

108
00:03:27,661 --> 00:03:29,383
your brain holds on to the last few

109
00:03:29,463 --> 00:03:32,425
ideas and links each new sentence to them.

110
00:03:33,286 --> 00:03:34,788
It builds a little model of what the

111
00:03:34,848 --> 00:03:35,568
text is saying.

112
00:03:36,429 --> 00:03:37,410
That chain is fragile.

113
00:03:37,950 --> 00:03:39,632
If your spotlight drifts for even a few

114
00:03:39,692 --> 00:03:41,794
sentences, a link goes missing.

115
00:03:42,555 --> 00:03:43,936
Then the next sentence makes no sense,

116
00:03:44,396 --> 00:03:45,297
and you have to go back.

117
00:03:46,038 --> 00:03:47,800
That's why you read the same paragraph three

118
00:03:47,860 --> 00:03:48,160
times.

119
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:50,381
The first thing pulling your spotlight is

120
00:03:50,421 --> 00:03:51,081
your own mind.

121
00:03:51,801 --> 00:03:53,062
When your attention drifts inward,

122
00:03:53,422 --> 00:03:55,743
a set of brain areas called the default

123
00:03:55,843 --> 00:03:57,963
mode network becomes more active.

124
00:03:58,824 --> 00:03:59,944
It's busy with memories,

125
00:04:00,224 --> 00:04:01,384
plans and daydreams.

126
00:04:02,265 --> 00:04:03,745
Mind wandering isn't a defect.

127
00:04:04,185 --> 00:04:05,606
It helps you plan and imagine.

128
00:04:06,246 --> 00:04:09,147
But while reading, it quietly steals the thread.

129
00:04:10,048 --> 00:04:11,928
And often you don't notice until a page

130
00:04:11,988 --> 00:04:12,268
later.

131
00:04:12,969 --> 00:04:14,849
The harder or more boring the text,

132
00:04:15,289 --> 00:04:16,890
the more often the mind slips away.

133
00:04:17,470 --> 00:04:19,692
The second pull comes from cues around you.

134
00:04:20,232 --> 00:04:21,853
A buzz or a ping is designed to

135
00:04:21,893 --> 00:04:23,094
grab attention, and it works.

136
00:04:23,874 --> 00:04:24,695
Even if you ignore it,

137
00:04:24,935 --> 00:04:26,376
your mind starts wondering who it was.

138
00:04:27,216 --> 00:04:29,138
In one experiment, students spent one week

139
00:04:29,158 --> 00:04:31,579
with phone alerts on and another with alerts

140
00:04:31,719 --> 00:04:31,860
off.

141
00:04:32,620 --> 00:04:35,122
With alerts on, they reported more inattention.

142
00:04:36,242 --> 00:04:38,064
What about a silent phone just sitting on

143
00:04:38,084 --> 00:04:39,785
your desk?

144
00:04:39,905 --> 00:04:41,746
A 2017 study claimed that even its mere

145
00:04:41,886 --> 00:04:43,387
presence drains your brainpower.

146
00:04:44,068 --> 00:04:46,389
But later, careful attempts to repeat that

147
00:04:46,469 --> 00:04:48,050
study did not find the effect.

148
00:04:48,430 --> 00:04:49,251
So it's the buzzing,

149
00:04:49,511 --> 00:04:50,512
more than the phone itself,

150
00:04:50,952 --> 00:04:52,333
that we have good evidence against.

151
00:04:52,953 --> 00:04:54,355
The third pull is switching.

152
00:04:55,056 --> 00:04:56,399
Opening a new tab feels like it takes

153
00:04:56,559 --> 00:04:57,120
one second.

154
00:04:57,480 --> 00:04:59,383
But coming back means reloading the chain

155
00:04:59,403 --> 00:05:00,445
of ideas you were holding.

156
00:05:01,146 --> 00:05:02,228
Part of your mind is still on the

157
00:05:02,308 --> 00:05:03,289
email you didn't finish.

158
00:05:03,930 --> 00:05:04,792
Do that twenty times,

159
00:05:04,832 --> 00:05:06,154
and forty minutes disappear.

160
00:05:06,734 --> 00:05:08,575
The fourth pull may be the screen itself.

161
00:05:09,415 --> 00:05:10,155
We do a lot of quick,

162
00:05:10,355 --> 00:05:11,575
shallow reading on screens:

163
00:05:11,875 --> 00:05:13,576
messages, feeds, headlines.

164
00:05:14,236 --> 00:05:15,917
One idea is that we carry that skimming

165
00:05:15,977 --> 00:05:17,817
habit over to everything we read on a

166
00:05:17,857 --> 00:05:18,197
screen.

167
00:05:18,877 --> 00:05:20,838
We read faster, and we check our own

168
00:05:20,898 --> 00:05:21,738
understanding less.

169
00:05:22,759 --> 00:05:24,539
That could explain why screen readers in the

170
00:05:24,579 --> 00:05:26,760
review felt more confident than they should

171
00:05:26,780 --> 00:05:27,000
have.

172
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:29,681
But this is still an idea being tested,

173
00:05:30,201 --> 00:05:31,121
not a settled fact.

174
00:05:31,701 --> 00:05:32,442
There's one more thing.

175
00:05:33,043 --> 00:05:35,066
When people do a single focused task for

176
00:05:35,106 --> 00:05:37,569
a long time, their performance tends to slip.

177
00:05:38,270 --> 00:05:39,752
In a small 2011 study,

178
00:05:40,113 --> 00:05:41,996
people who took two very short breaks from

179
00:05:42,036 --> 00:05:44,239
a fifty-minute task didn't show that slip.

180
00:05:45,020 --> 00:05:46,221
So stepping away for a moment,

181
00:05:46,442 --> 00:05:48,685
on purpose, can help you come back sharper.

182
00:05:49,245 --> 00:05:51,066
What about people who juggle many screens

183
00:05:51,126 --> 00:05:51,466
all day?

184
00:05:52,386 --> 00:05:55,248
A well-known 2009 study found that heavy media

185
00:05:55,288 --> 00:05:58,269
multitaskers were worse at ignoring distractions.

186
00:05:59,190 --> 00:05:59,890
It made headlines.

187
00:06:00,610 --> 00:06:02,111
But when other scientists tried to repeat

188
00:06:02,151 --> 00:06:03,512
it, the results were mixed.

189
00:06:04,292 --> 00:06:06,193
Pooling many studies, the link turned out

190
00:06:06,233 --> 00:06:08,154
to be small, and it may be even

191
00:06:08,294 --> 00:06:09,234
smaller than it looks.

192
00:06:09,975 --> 00:06:12,296
So we can't say that multitasking has rewired

193
00:06:12,456 --> 00:06:13,176
anyone's brain.

194
00:06:13,696 --> 00:06:15,097
Can you train your spotlight?

195
00:06:16,257 --> 00:06:17,418
In a 2013 study,

196
00:06:17,538 --> 00:06:19,719
forty-eight students took a two-week class.

197
00:06:20,500 --> 00:06:22,661
Half learned mindfulness: noticing when the

198
00:06:22,701 --> 00:06:24,642
mind drifts and gently bringing it back.

199
00:06:25,442 --> 00:06:27,083
The other half took a class on nutrition.

200
00:06:27,903 --> 00:06:30,104
Afterward, the mindfulness group's minds wandered

201
00:06:30,144 --> 00:06:32,206
less, and they scored higher on a reading

202
00:06:32,246 --> 00:06:33,086
comprehension test.

203
00:06:33,946 --> 00:06:34,787
It was a small study,

204
00:06:34,827 --> 00:06:37,288
but it suggests that noticing the drift is

205
00:06:37,348 --> 00:06:38,829
a skill you can practice.

206
00:06:39,449 --> 00:06:40,609
So, back to the question.

207
00:06:41,249 --> 00:06:42,590
The answer is C.

208
00:06:43,390 --> 00:06:45,490
The eight-second goldfish fact has no study

209
00:06:45,530 --> 00:06:45,891
behind it.

210
00:06:46,231 --> 00:06:47,991
And there's no solid evidence that your phone

211
00:06:48,011 --> 00:06:49,751
has permanently broken your brain.

212
00:06:50,632 --> 00:06:52,172
Your attention is a spotlight,

213
00:06:52,672 --> 00:06:54,573
and today's world keeps tugging it away.

214
00:06:55,073 --> 00:06:55,873
And it's not just you.

215
00:06:56,393 --> 00:06:58,594
Remember, people's minds drift nearly half

216
00:06:58,634 --> 00:07:00,174
the time, whatever they're doing.

217
00:07:00,714 --> 00:07:01,975
The good news is that you can make

218
00:07:02,015 --> 00:07:03,555
the spotlight easier to hold.

219
00:07:04,255 --> 00:07:05,856
Turn off notifications while you read,

220
00:07:06,296 --> 00:07:07,697
and put your phone out of reach.

221
00:07:08,597 --> 00:07:09,597
Keep one tab open.

222
00:07:10,138 --> 00:07:11,198
One task at a time.

223
00:07:11,918 --> 00:07:12,999
For text that really matters,

224
00:07:13,059 --> 00:07:15,680
consider paper, and give yourself enough time.

225
00:07:16,380 --> 00:07:17,440
When you notice you've drifted,

226
00:07:17,840 --> 00:07:19,481
go back to the last sentence you remember,

227
00:07:19,981 --> 00:07:20,781
not the whole page.

228
00:07:21,582 --> 00:07:24,143
And take short, planned breaks before your

229
00:07:24,183 --> 00:07:25,023
focus runs down.

230
00:07:25,623 --> 00:07:26,543
So, the next afternoon,

231
00:07:26,924 --> 00:07:27,544
you try again.

232
00:07:28,144 --> 00:07:29,865
Phone on silent, in the drawer.

233
00:07:30,485 --> 00:07:30,966
One tab.

234
00:07:31,486 --> 00:07:32,646
Twenty-five minutes of reading,

235
00:07:33,006 --> 00:07:34,287
then a two-minute stretch.

236
00:07:35,027 --> 00:07:37,048
By three o'clock, you're on the last page.

237
00:07:37,749 --> 00:07:39,650
And this time, you can actually remember what

238
00:07:39,710 --> 00:07:40,690
page one was about.

239
00:07:41,610 --> 00:07:43,311
And if your mind wandered during this video,

240
00:07:43,611 --> 00:07:44,192
that's normal.

241
00:07:44,872 --> 00:07:46,553
Just rewind ten seconds.
